    "ABBR": "OWEN",
    "Name": "Richard Owen Residence Hall",
    "OTHER_ABBR": " ",
    "OWNER": "PURDUE",
    "ADDRESS": "1160 W. Stadium Avenue",
    "BUILTYear": -378604800000,
    "OCCUPIEDYear": -444528000000,
    "RazeYear": 253402214399000,
    "Shape.STArea()": 8931.7933667199995,
    "Shape.STLength()": 750.48244664380229,
    "description": "Originally called the Men's Residence Hall H-1, the Richard Owen Residence Hall is home to over 700 Purdue students. It is located on the north side of campus near the Slayter Center for the Performing Arts and Ford Dining Court.",
    "buildingidlong": 25,
    "ChangeYear": 1514764800000,
    "constructionbeginyear": 1955,
    "constructionendyear": 1958,
    "cost": 3191100.0,
    "dedicationdate": "1965-06-05",
    "dedicationinfo": "Owen Residence Hall was named for Richard Owen, the first president of Purdue University. Mr. Owen was born in Scotland in 1810 and was the youngest of eight children. He immigrated to America and served in the U.S. Army during the Mexican War and the Civil War. He began working at Indiana University in 1864 and worked to establish the Agricultural and Normal school. The plans for this school became the first concept for Purdue University. He was also accomplished in the areas of geology, meteorology, and seismology.",
    "presidentatconstruction": "Frederick L. Hovde",
    "squarefootage": 157220
